#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148288} :activity/id #uuid "ddf081b5-4271-58f1-ac92-6ac95f2e57d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148747 :tutorial/content "A harmonic oscillator is said to be **forced** if it is subject to a nonzero external force $f(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nIf the external force $f(t)$ is periodic and its frequency is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, then the system experiences **resonance** and the amplitude of the oscillations increases without bound over time.\n\nFor example, consider the following differential equation which represents a harmonic oscillator subject to a forcing function $\\cos(2t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(2t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion,\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t).\n$$\n\nThe forcing function $f(t) = \\cos(2t)$ has the same frequency as the complementary solution above (both have a frequency of $\\Omega = 2 \\, \\textrm{s}^{-1}$), so the system experiences *resonance*. As a result,  the particular solution\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nhas an amplitude that is proportional to $t,$ which means that the amplitude will increase over time.\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote that the amplitude of his function grows linearly. The graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/9032f93f083a100b14f732ba114e306fd0c9c5e0576ce218c9248c0e140854de.png)\n\n**Important:** Not all forced oscillators experience resonance. In the above example, if we change the forcing function to $f(t) = \\cos(t),$ resulting in the equation\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0,\n$$\n\n then the amplitude of the particular solution is constant:\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t)\n$$\n\nThere is no resonance in this case since the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\cos(t)$ does not coincide with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\Omega = 2.$\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t)\\right).\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, the amplitude of the solution does not grow.\n\n![](images/0e/0eac5b659d3ea9712a3672eb443227d7c8cee5377ff52d7646ca263dc5e11007.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "77b16ea3-5ad1-5108-9c77-d314e6e9ddf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e32b8ecd-fee2-56e7-867d-2ac1f9f9b872" :step/math-academy-id 24279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147376 :question/id #uuid "bd1d2ef2-9472-5e57-b071-fbbaf80023da" :question/math-academy-id "e-6731"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048291}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434366}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "39e8e7e0-57a8-511a-aef2-6b002039cb19" :step/math-academy-id 24721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147377 :question/id #uuid "11a44451-5b9f-5892-b321-925b2a393551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434369}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "0dd1a25e-e81d-5bf2-9e52-1d14c32c9689" :step/math-academy-id 24722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147378 :question/id #uuid "aa77f4ef-67b6-5041-9a3b-1a36ffd5582d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050805}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434368}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "f42f709a-7c4f-5416-bf43-29a32c645bd5" :step/math-academy-id 24723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147379 :question/id #uuid "20b53dff-43b9-5256-ab7d-67aff56db408" :question/math-academy-id "e-6734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049099}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434370}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"} :step/id #uuid "dbf9225d-b92c-5540-96eb-190970598cab" :step/math-academy-id 24724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148766 :tutorial/content "Remember that, if an object of mass $m$ is attached to a spring with spring constant $k,$ and the damping coefficient is $c,$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nmy'' = -cy'-ky.\n$$\n\nIf there is an external force $F(t),$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab390ee6-c457-55d4-a265-9d62a1955ab8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4169 :tutorial/title "The Equation of Motion for a Forced Spring-Mass System"} :step/id #uuid "62673a11-379c-5680-872b-45a2328762ae" :step/math-academy-id 25340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148767 :tutorial/content "Given a forced harmonic motion equation *without* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nAlternatively, given a forced harmonic motion equation *with* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nif the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = p \\pm \\textrm{i}q,$ then the complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R e^{p t} \\cos{(q t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R e^{p t} \\sin{(q t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =e^{p t} \\left( A\\cos{q t} + B\\sin{q t} \\right)$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4683094-19f8-557e-b0c7-7aad98cab72c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4170 :tutorial/title "General Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "4be5106f-eac3-59d0-8ca0-5316fe821456" :step/math-academy-id 25341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148309}}] :activity/title "Forced Oscillators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147705}]}
